{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788225688,"nonce":"3wR3z9c10FMVJIwcQ9YwrfvD4ho=","title":"A silently clamped trade line reports full success, and buy_value/sell_value include the silver row so their difference is always zero","message":"Two sharp edges on the same surface, found by the first live run of `accept/3.5-dialog-verbs.py --phase 3`, session 11 (`65b03c2`), 2026-08-31. Both confirmed against the decompiled 1.6 source. Neither is the duplicate-line defect (`be75bc4`); they are independent.\n\n\n`trade-set {buy: 10030}` against a trader holding 31 returns `ok: true` with the line ACCEPTED, and the colony gets 31.\n\nWhy the existing guard does not fire, read from source: `RimWorld/Transferable.CanAdjustTo` returns `WasAccepted` **whenever `ClampAmount(destination) != CountToTransfer`**, and only returns `OverflowReport()` when the clamp EQUALS the current count. Starting from `countToTransfer == 0`, the game itself accepts, and `Transferable.AdjustTo` silently sets `ClampAmount(10030)` = 31.\n\n`TradeVerbs.TradeSet`'s Gate 4 calls exactly that predicate, so **the gate is correct and structurally unreachable from a zero row** — which is also why the no-red-error invariant held (`3.5e` passed; no \"Failed to adjust transferable counts\"). The gate is not the problem.\n\nThe problem is the ECHO. The accepted line carries `count` and `was` but no `requested` and no `clamped`, so asking for 10030 and getting 31 reads as complete success. Same failure class as `4087644`: the agent cannot tell \"did what I asked\" from \"did something else\".\n\n**Fix:** echo `requested` alongside `count` on every accepted line, and set an explicit `clamped: true` when they differ. Do not turn the clamp into a refusal without deciding that deliberately — clamping is the game's own behaviour and there is a real argument for keeping it; what is not defensible is not SAYING so.\n\nNote for whoever takes it: the gate DOES fire on a second identical call, because by then `ClampAmount(dest) == CountToTransfer`. So the reject row and its `min`/`max`/`requested`/`colony_has`/`trader_has` fields (Gate 4 → `Reject`) are already implemented and correct — they were simply never entered.\n\n\n`TradeVerbs.AddDealTotals` sums `CurTotalCurrencyCostForSource` / `ForDestination` over **all** tradeables — including the silver row. `RimWorld/TradeDeal.UpdateCurrencyCount` explicitly SKIPS `IsCurrency` when it does the same sum.\n\nBecause silver absorbs the difference, our two totals always equal each other, whatever the deal. Anything computing `sell_value - buy_value` from them gets 0 by construction and will look correct forever.\n\n**Fix:** exclude the currency row from `buy_value` / `sell_value` (and check `lines_with_action` for the same inclusion — it counts the silver row today, which is why a one-item deal reports 2). Callers wanting the cash effect should use `totals.colony_silver` and `totals.colony_silver_post_deal`, which are already published and are the honest pair.\n\n\n- An accepted line that was clamped carries `requested` and `clamped: true`; one that was not carries `clamped: false` or omits it consistently.\n- `buy_value` and `sell_value` exclude the currency row, and a deal with a known cash effect shows `sell_value - buy_value` equal to the actual silver delta rather than 0.\n- `lines_with_action` is documented as to whether it counts the silver row, and a live check asserts the number for a two-distinct-def deal so the ambiguity cannot return.\n- Zero red errors.\n\n\n- `Source/AutoRimmer/TradeVerbs.cs` — `TradeSet` Gate 4, the `accepted` echo, `AddDealTotals`.\n- `RimWorld/Transferable` — `CanAdjustTo`, `AdjustTo`, `ClampAmount`, `OverflowReport`.\n- `RimWorld/TradeDeal` — `UpdateCurrencyCount` and its `IsCurrency` skip.","files":null}]}